 > [!NOTE]
 > The `secure: true` option tells the browser to only store and send the cookie over HTTPS. When developing locally over plain HTTP, compliant browsers (notably Safari and iOS, and Chrome on some local domains) silently drop the cookie, so the session will not persist. Set `cookie: { secure: false }` during local development to work around this.
 
+## Rotate the Password
+
+Changing `password` invalidates every session sealed with the old one. To rotate without signing users out, pass a list with the new password first and the old one after it:
+
+```ts
+const session = await useSession(event, {
+  password: [process.env.SESSION_PASSWORD_NEW, process.env.SESSION_PASSWORD_OLD],
+});
+```
+
+The first password seals, and every password in the list, in order, can unseal. A session read from a cookie that was sealed with a password other than the first is resealed with the first one in the same request, so the `Set-Cookie` header appears on requests that did not have one before. Sessions read from the session header are not resealed.
+
+> [!NOTE]
+> Like any reseal, this one can race with concurrent requests that carry the same cookie. See the note on concurrent requests under [Expiration](#expiration).
+
+Once the old sessions have been resealed, remove the old password from the list. Keep the list short: a session that no password opens costs one PBKDF2 derivation per password. A compromised password left in the list keeps sessions forgeable. The list must not be empty, and each password must be at least 32 characters.
+
